==English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|scondrel}}, {{m|en|scondrell}}, {{m|en|skoundrell}}, {{m|en|skowndrell}}, of {{unk|en|title=unknown}} origin. Possibly related to northern English or Scottish {{m|en|scunner|t=to shrink back in fear or loathing}} (Encyclopædia Britannica 1911).

===Pronunciation===
*{{IPA|en|/ˈskaʊ̯ndɹəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-scoundrel.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[mean]], [[worthless]] [[fellow]]; a [[rascal]]; a [[villain]]; a person without [[honor]] or [[virtue]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:villain}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Man|IV|14|lines=201–202|Go! if your ancient but ignoble blood / Has crept thro' '''Scoundrels''' ever ſince the Flood.}}

